      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "When to Choose SGLT2 Inhibitors"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "SGLT2 inhibitors block glucose reabsorption in the kidneys, passing extra sugar out through urine. Clinical guidelines from organizations like the American College of Cardiology and diabetes associations favor SGLT2 inhibitors if you have specific organ risks:",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "American College of Cardiology",
            "link": "https://www.acc.org"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Heart Failure: They significantly lower the risk of hospitalization and death from heart failure, regardless of whether you have reduced or preserved ejection fraction."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD): They slow the progression of kidney disease and protect renal function."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Convenience Preference: They are entirely oral pills taken once a day."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "When to Choose GLP-1 Receptor Agonists"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "GLP-1 receptor agonists mimic natural hormones to slow digestion, stimulate insulin release, and signal fullness to the brain. They are often prioritized when different clinical outcomes are the priority:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Significant Weight Loss & Obesity: They provide superior weight reduction compared to SGLT2 inhibitors."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Atherosclerotic Cardiovascular Disease & Stroke: They are highly effective at reducing major adverse cardiovascular events, such as heart attack and stroke risk."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Higher Blood Sugar Reduction: They generally lower hemoglobin A1C more effectively than SGLT2 inhibitors."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Combination Therapy"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "In many cases, health providers do not view these as an \"either/or\" choice. Studies show that combining an SGLT2 inhibitor and a GLP-1 receptor agonist provides complementary benefits for blood sugar, weight, blood pressure, and comprehensive heart-kidney protection."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Talk to your doctor or endocrinologist to review your kidney function, heart health, and personal preferences to determine the best choice or combination for your treatment plan."
      }
    ],
